            "text": "**My Analysis of the Prime Number Question**\n\nOkay, let's break this down. First, I see the target number: 391. The prompt wants to know if it's prime and to answer with a \"yes\" or \"no\" followed by *one* sentence explaining why. That's straightforward enough. No ambiguity there.\n\nNow, to determine if 391 is prime. Time to do the math. I need to check for divisibility. Square root of 391 is roughly 19.77, so I need to check primes up to 19. I'll go through the usual suspects: 2, 3, 5, 7, 11, 13, 17, and 19.\n\nIt's odd, so no division by 2. Sum of digits is 13, so not divisible by 3. Doesn't end in a 0 or 5, so not divisible by 5. Let's start the divisions: 391 divided by 7 doesn't work. Same for 11 and 13. Hmm... Oh! 391 divided by 17 gives me 23! That's it, 17 multiplied by 23 is 391, so 391 is composite, not prime.\n\nOkay, time to craft the response. The answer is \"No.\" The explanation needs to be concise and accurate, and follow the one-sentence constraint. I can say, \"No, 391 is not a prime number because it is divisible by 17 and 23 (17 × 23 = 391).\" Alternatively, \"No, 391 is not a prime number because it is a composite number divisible by 17 and 23.\" That works perfectly; it's concise, accurate, and satisfies the prompt's requirements. Done.\n\n\n",
